body:
The signature rendering only special-cases VAR_POSITIONAL/VAR_KEYWORD via numeric kind values (2/4). This drops important signature markers like positional-only ("/") and keyword-only ("*") parameters, so generated stubs can end up with an incorrect callable interface (e.g. `def f(a, b)` instead of `def f(a, *, b)`). Consider building the parameter list from `inspect.Parameter` kinds (using `Parameter.VAR_POSITIONAL`, `Parameter.VAR_KEYWORD`, `Parameter.KEYWORD_ONLY`, `Parameter.POSITIONAL_ONLY`) and inserting the required `*`/`/` separators, or reusing `str(signature)` and then removing a leading `self`/`cls` safely. Also avoid magic numbers if possible to keep this portable across inspect implementations.
```suggestion
                        sig_str = str(sig)
                        # Expect a string like "(a, *, b)" or "(self, /, a, *args, **kwargs)"
                        if sig_str.startswith("(") and sig_str.endswith(")"):
                            sig_str = sig_str[1:-1]
                        # Ignore unusable signatures like "(...)"
                        if sig_str.strip() not in ("...",):
                            if in_class > 0:
                                # Remove leading self/cls if present in the inspected signature
                                if sig_str.startswith("self, "):
                                    sig_str = sig_str[6:]
                                elif sig_str.startswith("cls, "):
                                    sig_str = sig_str[5:]
                                elif sig_str == "self" or sig_str == "cls":
                                    sig_str = ""
                                # Always include a leading self for class members
                                if sig_str:
                                    params = "self, " + sig_str
                                else:
                                    params = "self"
                            else:
                                params = sig_str
